1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,940
One morning you wake up and find his food
still in the bowl.

2
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:05,120
You search the house but can't find him.

3
00:00:05,390 --> 00:00:09,000
It feels strange because he isn't the type
to run away or anything like that.

4
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:14,000
Until finally you see that your dog is
isolating himself, hiding under the bed so

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,120
you won't see him and avoiding eye
contact.

6
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:18,640
You ask yourself, did I do something
wrong?

7
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:20,180
What's happening to him?

8
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,700
Until an even more terrifying question
arises.

9
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:25,360
Does my dog know what's happening to him?

10
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:26,160
Is he afraid?

11
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:27,490
Is he in pain?

12
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:28,520
Did I do enough?

13
00:00:28,521 --> 00:00:32,203
For a long time, it's been
believed that when dogs are about

14
00:00:32,204 --> 00:00:35,360
to die, they tend to wander
off or enter a stage of anxiety.

15
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:37,620
But that's not exactly the case.

16
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:40,280
The truth is, we know very little about
our pets.

17
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:42,819
This video is going to
be a bit different from the

18
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:44,880
rest because we're talking
about something deeper.

19
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:46,800
The final days of our beloved pets.

20
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:49,900
But I want you to understand that this
isn't dark or sad.

21
00:00:50,170 --> 00:00:53,600
Rather, from a psychological perspective,
it's actually quite comforting.

22
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:57,160
Your dog doesn't know he's dying,
but he knows that something important,

23
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:58,500
something much more significant,
is going to happen to him.

24
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:02,680
Something that might mean never seeing
each other again.

25
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:05,310
Answering the question,
do our dogs actually

26
00:01:05,311 --> 00:01:08,021
know they are going to
cross the rainbow bridge?

27
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:10,020
The answer isn't as simple as yes or no.

28
00:01:10,490 --> 00:01:14,820
In 2021, a philosopher named Susana
Monceau from the University of Madrid in

29
00:01:14,821 --> 00:01:18,220
Spain published a study in a journal
called Urkentis.

30
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:21,820
In it, she proposed an idea that shifted
the perception of many scientists,

31
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:26,140
explaining that among many species,
dogs possess a minimal concept of death.

32
00:01:26,710 --> 00:01:28,500
However, having a concept of death is
difficult.

33
00:01:28,501 --> 00:01:31,900
It's not the same as having a perception
of it, let alone one's own.

34
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,140
Humans are the only species that think
about what happens after we exist.

35
00:01:36,765 --> 00:01:39,080
Other species lack that specific
reasoning.

36
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:41,340
They don't think deeply about what comes
next.

37
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:45,980
What they do instead is something more
immediate and, in its own way, more honest.

38
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:49,860
They feel that their bodies no longer
respond the way they used to, that walks

39
00:01:49,861 --> 00:01:51,920
are harder, that their appetite has
changed.

40
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,780
But I think the most interesting part of
the case is, they can smell it.

41
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:57,860
It's no secret that dogs have a
superpower.

42
00:01:57,861 --> 00:01:59,380
But when it comes to scent.

43
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:02,020
Thanks to their 300 million olfactory
receptors.

44
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:05,660
There's a common urban legend about how
they can smell cancer.

45
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:07,700
A legend that is entirely true.

46
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:11,166
In California, five ordinary
household dogs were trained

47
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:13,720
to detect lung cancer in
samples on human breath.

48
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:16,380
We're talking about 99% accuracy.

49
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:22,180
A 2024 study at the Penn Vet Working Dog
Center showed that dogs can detect cancer

50
00:02:22,181 --> 00:02:25,440
in the saliva of other dogs with 90%
sensitivity.

51
00:02:26,090 --> 00:02:27,840
This means that, logically, they were able
to smell cancer.

52
00:02:27,841 --> 00:02:30,520
They were constantly analyzing their own
chemical signature.

53
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:33,500
From their own scent to every breath they
take.

54
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:37,080
Detecting compounds and everything going
on inside them.

55
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:39,980
They have the ability to detect when
something is failing.

56
00:02:40,410 --> 00:02:42,930
Understanding all of this,
we can see how dogs are

57
00:02:42,931 --> 00:02:45,620
essentially beings that can
sense when something is wrong.

58
00:02:46,110 --> 00:02:49,580
When that happens, and I think most of us
have been in this situation, they either

59
00:02:49,581 --> 00:02:52,940
start to distance themselves, hiding in
corners or under the bed.

60
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:57,820
Or they do the exact
opposite and develop an
61
00:02:57,821 --> 00:02:57,820
incredibly strong
attachment to each other.

61
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:01,380
Most people interpret the former as
sadness and the latter as love.

62
00:03:01,870 --> 00:03:03,963
However, I believe
science has an answer that

63
00:03:03,964 --> 00:03:05,900
is more interesting
and even more moving.

64
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,140
In 1988, Benjamin Hart, from the UC Davis
School of Veterinary Medicine,

65
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:13,960
published a paper that became fundamental
to understanding sick animals.

66
00:03:14,645 --> 00:03:17,900
He demonstrated that isolation,
loss of appetite, increased sleep,

67
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,880
and social withdrawal are not signs of
giving up.

68
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:24,220
They are evolutionary traits inherited
through centuries of growth.

69
00:03:25,050 --> 00:03:26,296
They aren't in a state of depression.

70
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:27,800
Instead, they shut down all of their
emotions.

71
00:03:27,801 --> 00:03:29,315
They are in a state of
mental and physical activity

72
00:03:29,316 --> 00:03:31,301
to focus all their energy
on fighting the illness.

73
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:34,400
Hart called this the animal's total effort
to overcome.

74
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:38,480
A technical way of saying, I'm going to
use everything I've got.

75
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:40,500
But there's also a second factor.

76
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:45,440
In ancient times, when dogs were more wolf
than dog, sick or elderly animals would

77
00:03:45,441 --> 00:03:47,840
move away from the pack to draw away
predators.

78
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:51,120
This was to avoid being a burden and to
help their own.

79
00:03:51,510 --> 00:03:53,980
Preventing the spread of disease or
slowing the group down.

80
00:03:54,390 --> 00:03:57,780
Everything we've discussed helps us
understand more deeply why they behave.

81
00:03:58,690 --> 00:03:59,731
It's part of their nature.

82
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:02,500
A strange way of saying you are one of
their own.

83
00:04:02,875 --> 00:04:07,220
Whether they draw closer or pull away,
it stems from that biological need to

84
00:04:07,221 --> 00:04:10,880
protect, to inform, and in turn to express
their loyalty.

85
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:13,980
Pain is overwhelming, confusing,
and conflicting.

86
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:18,660
When a dog is afraid, he runs straight to
his protector, which will always be you.

87
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:22,140
But when he is certain of things,
he moves away to look out for you.

88
00:04:22,470 --> 00:04:24,480
We just have to know how to tell the
difference.

89
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:28,560
Specialists explain that sudden intense
attachment to the dog, combined with

90
00:04:28,735 --> 00:04:32,177
restlessness or panting,
often indicates uncontrolled

91
00:04:32,178 --> 00:04:35,140
pain, which should always
be evaluated by a vet.

92
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:36,720
But the attachment itself?

93
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:38,180
That isn't desperation.

94
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:39,610
That is trust.

95
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:42,660
We've all had similar experiences with a
dog we've owned.

96
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:45,080
Or if not, it's something that eventually
happens.

97
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:48,880
He visits every family member,
one by one, on his last day.

98
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:52,420
He walks to his favorite spot in the yard
that he hadn't visited in months.

99
00:04:53,085 --> 00:04:56,480
Or after days of barely moving,
he suddenly gets up, eats dinner,

100
00:04:56,830 --> 00:05:00,220
wags his tail, and seems completely normal
for an hour.

101
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:01,340
And then he's gone.

102
00:05:01,730 --> 00:05:03,240
This is known as the last rally.

103
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:05,700
It's a pattern that can look different
every time.

104
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:08,180
But it begins with a sudden burst of
energy.

105
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,141
This isn't just a random occurrence.

106
00:05:10,745 --> 00:05:14,152
In 2023, a group of researchers
cataloged 114 of these

107
00:05:14,153 --> 00:05:17,500
accounts from owners and
veterinarians to find this answer.

108
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,640
One veterinarian even noted that it wasn't
uncommon to be called to put a dog down,

109
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:26,260
only to be greeted at the door by an
animal barking and jumping.

110
00:05:26,635 --> 00:05:27,540
A team led by Jim, a veterinarian,
and a veterinarian, and a veterinarian,

111
00:05:27,541 --> 00:05:30,540
and a veterinarian, Jimo Borjigan at the
University of Michigan discovered that

112
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:35,180
when the brain loses oxygen during cardiac
arrest, it doesn't simply shut off.

113
00:05:35,690 --> 00:05:37,638
Instead, it produces
a surge of organized

114
00:05:37,639 --> 00:05:40,821
gamma wave activity
in the posterior cortex.

115
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:43,854
They found that in humans,
this is a final burst of neural

116
00:05:43,855 --> 00:05:47,100
coherence as the brain tries
one last time to restore balance.

117
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:50,100
Who's to say the same thing doesn't happen
to dogs?

118
00:05:50,690 --> 00:05:53,100
It might be something in nature,
it might be biological.

119
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:57,520
The point is that dogs use their last
ounce of energy to eat what they want.

120
00:05:57,521 --> 00:06:02,220
You offer them to look at your face one
more time and to reaffirm their bond.

121
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:04,540
Both things can be true at the same time.

122
00:06:05,010 --> 00:06:07,740
The explanation can be science and the
meaning can be real.

123
00:06:08,190 --> 00:06:11,152
This is the hardest moment,
the one anyone would fear, but it

124
00:06:11,153 --> 00:06:13,960
doesn't have to be bad and it
doesn't have to be traumatic.

125
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:17,860
If you're carrying guilt over a situation
like this, keep watching.

126
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:22,080
Some people make the decision,
one that is never easy and always painful,

127
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:23,920
to choose euthanasia for their pet.

128
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:25,560
This is not a bad thing.

129
00:06:25,785 --> 00:06:29,280
In fact, when there are no other decisions
left, it might be the kindest act.

130
00:06:29,770 --> 00:06:33,600
A single dose essentially flips a switch,
turning off the brain and the system,

131
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:35,500
specifically the consciousness.

132
00:06:36,275 --> 00:06:38,060
Of course, it's a more complex process.

133
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:42,680
The animal loses brain activity about 52
seconds after the injection, falling into

134
00:06:42,681 --> 00:06:46,200
a deep sleep where they don't actually
feel the moment they lose consciousness.

135
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:51,260
This was proven in 2015 by a team at UC
Davis led by Monica Ailman.

136
00:06:51,670 --> 00:06:55,120
And yes, it's known that following those
minutes, there may be movements,

137
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:58,800
reactions, or gasps that seem like they
are suffering.

138
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:00,500
But that isn't the case.

139
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,640
It is merely a muscular reaction.

140
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:04,340
They are already gone.

141
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:05,480
They aren't suffering.

142
00:07:05,850 --> 00:07:09,380
We know the heart can continue to beat for
up to 16 minutes afterward.

143
00:07:09,950 --> 00:07:11,440
But you have nothing to worry about.

144
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:14,060
They passed in the most peaceful way
possible.

145
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:17,720
The process isn't traumatic during or
before the injection, either.

146
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:19,521
For them, things work differently.

147
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:23,320
They don't perceive that something good or
bad is being done to them.

148
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:27,500
In fact, at the Frey E
University in Berlin, they
150
00:07:27,501 --> 00:07:27,500
monitored over 40% of
euthanasia in their pets.

149
00:07:27,501 --> 00:07:32,340
They found that stress markers remained
low at all times, whether the owner was

150
00:07:32,341 --> 00:07:35,800
present or not, and whether they were at
home or at the vet.

151
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,080
The concept of euthanasia simply has no
meaning to them.

152
00:07:39,450 --> 00:07:40,740
But here is what did matter.

153
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,920
And this is the part we want you to
remember.

154
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:48,100
As we discussed in our first video,
fMRI research by Gregory Burns at Emory

155
00:07:48,101 --> 00:07:53,697
University showed that the caudate
nucleus activates more strongly

156
00:07:53,698 --> 00:07:57,080
at the scent of a familiar
owner than at any other stimulus.

157
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:58,320
Not food.

158
00:07:58,440 --> 00:07:59,120
Not other dogs.

159
00:07:59,360 --> 00:07:59,660
You.

160
00:08:00,170 --> 00:08:03,180
And that activation happens even when the
owner isn't visible.

161
00:08:03,610 --> 00:08:05,120
The scent alone is enough.

162
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:09,400
What this means is that in those final
seconds of consciousness, if you are

163
00:08:09,401 --> 00:08:13,420
there, your scent, your voice,
and your hand on their fur are registered

164
00:08:13,421 --> 00:08:16,700
in the part of your dog's brain associated
with safety and reward.

165
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:19,220
You are the last good thing they
experience.

166
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:20,500
Not the pain.

167
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:21,440
Not the fear.

168
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:22,140
You.

169
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:24,300
The science on this is not ambiguous.

170
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:27,120
Your presence is the greatest gift you can
give them.

171
00:08:27,675 --> 00:08:30,180
And if that brings you any comfort,
hold onto it.

172
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:31,240
Because it's real.

173
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:32,860
And then comes the hardest part.

174
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:33,720
Grief.

175
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:37,960
Anyone who has ever had a pet knows this
is more than just a passing feeling.

176
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:39,920
It's something that demands respect.

177
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,500
For many people, the loss of a pet can be
incredibly distressing.

178
00:08:43,910 --> 00:08:46,560
For some, it is exactly like losing a
human loved one.

179
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,220
Grief and the reaction to it are different
for everyone.

180
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:51,560
But it isn't an isolated case.

181
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:53,080
Nor is it merely subjective.

182
00:08:53,850 --> 00:08:58,740
In 2016, the journal PLOS ONE published an
article from a Maynooth University in

183
00:08:58,741 --> 00:09:04,420
Ireland, led by Philip Hyland, where he
evaluated 975 people who had lost a pet.

184
00:09:04,870 --> 00:09:08,800
Using clinical techniques for prolonged
grief disorder, the results were striking.

185
00:09:09,290 --> 00:09:15,800
7.5% met all the diagnostic criteria,
while a national survey by the RSPCA found

186
00:09:15,801 --> 00:09:20,680
that 75% of pet owners begin grieving
before the animal even passes away.

187
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:23,520
They call this anticipatory grief.

188
00:09:24,090 --> 00:09:26,288
Furthermore, less
than 7% felt that the

189
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:28,841
people around them
took their pain seriously.

190
00:09:29,210 --> 00:09:31,380
More than half hid how they were truly
feeling.

191
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:34,480
Psychologists call this disenfranchised
grief.

192
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:37,413
The pain that society
tells you isn't valid,

193
00:09:37,414 --> 00:09:40,261
but your brain doesn't
care what society says.

194
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:44,640
Researchers from Harvard and Massachusetts
General Hospital scanned the brains of

195
00:09:44,641 --> 00:09:48,180
mothers looking at photos of their own
children and their own dogs.

196
00:09:48,635 --> 00:09:52,511
The core emotional network,
the amygdala, the orbitofrontal

197
00:09:52,512 --> 00:09:55,700
cortex, the dorsal pituitary
gland, activated in both cases.

198
00:09:56,290 --> 00:09:59,960
The attachment circuit doesn't check the
species, it responds to the bond.

199
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:03,640
As we've discussed through this series,
the relationship between a dog and their

200
00:10:03,641 --> 00:10:07,900
person is written in stress hormones,
heart rate patterns, and even the gut

201
00:10:07,901 --> 00:10:10,400
bacteria they literally share from living
together.

202
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:13,540
When that bond is broken, grief isn't just
sentimental.

203
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:16,989
It's your nervous system
registering the loss of a

204
00:10:16,990 --> 00:10:20,040
biological companion whose
body was synchronized with yours.

205
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:22,840
And that deserves to be honored,
not minimized.

206
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:26,280
So, here's what the science actually says
when you put all of it together.

207
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:28,160
A dog does not fear death.

208
00:10:28,380 --> 00:10:29,620
They do not lie in dread.

209
00:10:30,190 --> 00:10:31,400
They do not count the hours.

210
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:35,360
What they carry, instead, is something
that every brain scan confirms.

211
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:39,220
A neurological portrait of you,
stored in their reward circuits,

212
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:43,120
that activates every time they catch your
scent or hear your voice.

213
00:10:43,910 --> 00:10:45,140
You are not just their owner.

214
00:10:45,470 --> 00:10:46,830
You are their favorite experience.

215
00:10:47,450 --> 00:10:48,450
That's not poetry.

216
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:49,500
That's data.

217
00:10:49,610 --> 00:10:53,600
And the life you gave them, the walks,
the couch time, the mornings, the way you

218
00:10:53,601 --> 00:10:56,200
said their name, all of
it built a welfare history

219
00:10:56,201 --> 00:10:59,080
that veterinary science
would classify as good.

220
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:01,375
Not because every day
was perfect, but because

221
00:11:01,376 --> 00:11:04,081
the balance was
overwhelmingly positive.

222
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:05,220
You gave them that.

223
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:08,540
If you're in this moment now, or if it's
coming, be there.

224
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:11,260
Your presence matters, down to the
neurochemistry.

225
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:15,320
And if it already happened, and you're
still carrying the weight of it, know this.

226
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:19,760
It is the biologically accurate,
proportional cost of having been someone's

227
00:11:19,860 --> 00:11:22,840
entire world, and having them be part of
yours.

228
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:26,100
If this video reached you at the right
time, or it helped you understand

229
00:11:26,101 --> 00:11:26,740
something, let me know in the comments
below.

230
00:11:26,741 --> 00:11:28,420
And I'll see you next time.

